.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt WSIM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm wsim
.Nd the software Wi-Fi radio and its medium
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/wsim
.Pp
.Cd rach2.wsim=N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A software radio is a 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi radio that the kernel's station layer
drives as it drives a chip, as Linux's
.Sy mac80211_hwsim
is for mac80211.
It sends and receives whole 802.11 frames, with no FCS, and has no key
slots, so that the station encrypts and decrypts in software.
Its medium is a program that opens
.Pa /dev/wsim ,
reads the frames the radio sends and writes the frames it receives, such as
.Xr wsimrelay 8 .
.Pp
Each open of
.Pa /dev/wsim
gives the descriptor one radio, wsim
.Ar N ,
which the station layer registers as a phy with a wlan interface: the
lowest-numbered
radio the boot line made that no descriptor carries, or else a new one,
removed again at the descriptor's last close.
.Cd rach2.wsim=N ,
.Ar N
from 1 to 4, makes radios wsim0 to wsim
.Ar N Ns \-1
at boot, which stay when their descriptors close and refuse frames while
none carries them.
A machine has at most four radios, the registry's four interfaces.
Radio
.Ar N
has the address 02:77:73:00:00:
.Ar N ,
channels 1 to 14 before the regulatory rules, the legacy rates from 1 to
54 Mbit/s, and HT MCS 0 to 7 at 20 MHz with a short guard interval.
.Ss Records
A
.Xr read 2
gives one frame the radio sent, and a
.Xr write 2
takes one record, each a record of version 1 of the relay format, which
the wireless Readme defines: a 40-byte header of big-endian fields, then
the frame.
.Pp
A frame the radio sent has its radio number, the flag TX, NO_ACK when no
acknowledgement was asked or the frame is group addressed, the channel's
frequency in MHz, the first rate of its series, as 100 kbit/s or an MCS
index with the MCS flag, and a nonzero cookie of the radio's own.
A read waits for one unless the descriptor is non-blocking.
.Pp
A frame written is received by the radio if it is running and tuned to
the record's frequency, and its filters pass it: a unicast frame only for
its own address and a control frame only when the station asks for them,
unless the radio is promiscuous.
Its signal is the record's, in dBm, and its TSF the record's, or the
radio's own when the record gives 0.
The write returns the record's length for a frame received, and 0 for one
the radio did not receive, whether stopped, elsewhere, filtered, or with
its receive queue full: that is the medium losing a frame, not an error.
.Pp
A transmit status written completes the frame of its cookie once, with
one outcome: ACKED, NO_ACK or FAILED, and the attempts made, 0 when
unknown.
A frame that waits for its status when the radio stops, or when its
descriptor closes, fails.
.Pp
.Xr poll 2
reports the device readable while a frame waits, and always writable.
.Ss Requests
.Bl -tag -width WSIM_IOC_INFO
.It Dv WSIM_IOC_INFO
0x80107701, fills a
.Vt struct wsim_info
of sixteen bytes in the host's order: the radio number and the frequency
it is tuned to, 0 before the first, as two 16-bit integers; its six-byte
address; a byte that is 1 while the station runs it; a byte of its
receive filters; and four zero bytes.
.El
.Pp
The device is root's to open, mode 0600.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A non-blocking read with no frame waiting.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A read into a buffer smaller than the next record, which stays; a written
record that the format refuses, names another radio, or has a frequency,
rate or MCS the radio lacks.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
An open before the station layer is up.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A status whose cookie names no frame that waits.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
An open while four radios are carried.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another ioctl.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr wsimrelay 8
